<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Mac App Store distribution REQUIRES the App Sandbox. The dev bundle
         from xbox/tools/build-macos-app.sh signs against this same file, so
         the game you play at your desk is sandboxed exactly like the one a
         reviewer opens — a sandbox violation has nowhere to hide until
         review. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Outbound only, three destinations, all of them in main.swift:
         • NetworkBridge.saveReplay — HTTPS POST of a finished match to
           aesthetic.computer/api/oskiewar-replays.
         • NetworkBridge.publishLive — wss:// publisher socket on
           session-server.aesthetic.computer so spectators can watch a round.
         • OscSender.send — UDP OSC on 51338, broadcast, so a DAW on the same
           desk can score the fight from the game's own events.
         The app never listens for a connection, so network.server stays off.
         The broadcast is also what NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription in
         Info.plist explains: macOS 15+ asks the player before the first
         packet leaves for the LAN, and a denial costs the OSC feed and
         nothing else. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- oskiewar.js IS the game — the whole match loop runs inside
         JavaScriptCore, every frame. On Apple silicon JSC only gets its JIT
         when the process carries this key; without it the interpreter takes
         over. Measured on this machine with a JSC hot loop: 665 ms without,
         151 ms with — 4.4x. A 60fps fighter cannot pay that. Permitted in
         Mac App Store builds (unlike its unsigned-memory siblings below). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- NOT included, and why:
         • com.apple.security.device.bluetooth — game controllers come in
           through the high-level GameController framework
           (GCController.startWirelessControllerDiscovery); there is no
           CoreBluetooth anywhere in main.swift, so pads pair and read fine
           without it. Menu Band shipped this key and App Review pulled it
           under 2.4.5(i) in Jul 2026 for exactly this reason.
         • com.apple.security.device.audio-input — NativeAudio only ever
           attaches AVAudioPlayerNodes to the main mixer. The engine's input
           node is never touched, so there is no microphone to declare.
         • com.apple.security.files.* — the game writes no files at all. A
           replay is POSTed, not saved; the only reads are its own bundled
           oskiewar.js, qr.mjs, the Comic Relief face, and the icon, and a
           sandboxed app can always read its own bundle.
         • com.apple.security.network.server — nothing binds or accepts.
         • com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory and
           com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation — JSC needs
           neither once allow-jit is granted, and both draw review scrutiny
           that allow-jit does not. -->
</dict>
</plist>
